    {
      "id": "0006",
      "front": "Evaluate $\\int e^x \\sin x\\,dx$.",
      "back": "$\\frac{1}{2}e^x(\\sin x - \\cos x) + c$",
      "answerType": "EXPRESSION",
      "keyPoints": [
        {
          "text": "integrate by parts twice",
          "synonyms": [
            "the same integral reappears"
          ],
          "mustInclude": true
        },
        {
          "text": "solve the resulting equation for the integral",
          "synonyms": [
            "2I = e^x (sin x - cos x)"
          ],
          "mustInclude": true
        },
        {
          "text": "(1/2) e^x (sin x - cos x) + c",
          "mustInclude": true
        }
      ],
      "mcq": {
        "correct": "$\\frac{1}{2}e^x(\\sin x - \\cos x) + c$",
        "distractors": [
          "$e^x(\\sin x + \\cos x) + c$",
          "$\\frac{1}{2}e^x(\\sin x + \\cos x) + c$",
          "$e^x \\cos x + c$"
        ]
      },
      "explanation": "Two rounds of integration by parts bring the original integral back with a minus sign: $I = -e^x\\cos x + e^x\\sin x - I$, so $2I = e^x(\\sin x - \\cos x)$. The same method works for $e^x\\cos x$. The trap is choosing a different $u$ in the second round, which undoes the first and produces $0 = 0$ instead of an equation.",
      "reviewStatus": "AI_UNREVIEWED",
      "specRef": "Further Pure 2: Further integration",
      "tagRefs": [
        "edexcel",
        "a-level",
        "further-maths",
        "further-pure-2"
      ]
