      "back": "$x\\ln x - x + c$",
      "answerType": "EXPRESSION",
      "keyPoints": [
        {
          "text": "take u = ln x and dv/dx = 1",
          "synonyms": [
            "integration by parts with a hidden 1"
          ],
          "mustInclude": true
        },
        {
          "text": "x ln x - integral of 1 dx",
          "synonyms": [
            "the remaining integral is just x"
          ],
          "mustInclude": true
        },
        {
          "text": "x ln x - x + c",
          "mustInclude": true
        }
      ],
      "mcq": {
        "correct": "$x\\ln x - x + c$",
        "distractors": [
          "$\\frac{1}{x} + c$",
          "$x\\ln x + x + c$",
          "$\\frac{(\\ln x)^2}{2} + c$"
        ]
      },
      "explanation": "Integrating by parts with $u = \\ln x$ and $\\frac{dv}{dx} = 1$ gives $x\\ln x - \\int x \\times \\frac{1}{x}\\,dx = x\\ln x - x + c$. The trick is seeing the invisible factor of 1. Check by differentiation: $\\ln x + 1 - 1 = \\ln x$. The last distractor is what you get by pretending $\\ln x$ is a power of $\\ln x$.",
      "reviewStatus": "AI_UNREVIEWED",
      "specRef": "Further Pure 2: Further integration",
      "tagRefs": [
